--TEST--
A closure written in a `Core\Html::later` call cannot change the response head -- a status, a header, a redirect, a cookie, a body method or a session regeneration is a compile error there, in a nested closure too, while the same calls in the main script and an `echo` in the closure compile -- `rule:core-classes/html-later`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A `later` closure fills one part of the page. At run time each of these
// calls throws `LogicError` inside it, and where the checker sees the closure
// written in place, each is a compile error instead.
Core\Response::setStatus(200);
var $slot = Core\Html::later(fn(): void => {
    Core\Response::setStatus(404);
    Core\Response::setHeader("X-Shop", "1");
    Core\Response::redirect("/shop");
    Core\Response::addCookie("sid", "abc");
    Core\Response::text("body");
    Core\Session::regenerate();
});

// A closure nested in a `later` closure runs in the same slot.
var $nested = Core\Html::later(fn(): void => {
    var $inner = fn(): void => { Core\Response::json(1); };
});

// The accepted half: an `echo` in the closure is the slot's own output, and
// the options beside the closure are not part of it.
var $page = Core\Html::later(fn(): void => { echo "<p>Blog</p>"; },
                             {placeholder: html`<p>Loading</p>`});
Core\Response::setHeader("X-Blog", "1");
--EXPECT--

